WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:04.840
All right, my friends.

00:04.840 --> 00:08.120
So now let's have a little break in order to have a real talk about the CTE.

00:08.240 --> 00:09.560
But first, some coffee.

00:11.160 --> 00:16.280
And now I can see that I'm working with SQL since really long, long time ago, over 15 years.

00:16.320 --> 00:20.560
And I can say as well, I have met a lot of SQL developers in different projects.

00:20.560 --> 00:25.440
And if there is one thing that all those SQL developers love is the CTE.

00:25.720 --> 00:27.520
They love using it everywhere.

00:27.520 --> 00:31.640
Like each time they write a query, they gonna be writing SQL, CTE.

00:31.880 --> 00:32.760
And of course it's fine.

00:32.760 --> 00:33.960
It's not a bad thing.

00:33.960 --> 00:36.400
But the problem with that, they overuse it.

00:36.440 --> 00:41.520
Of course not all of them, but a lot of SQL developers overuse using the CTE.

00:41.720 --> 00:46.680
Of course, the CTE is very powerful, but with power comes great responsibility.

00:47.160 --> 00:51.800
Remember, with great power comes great responsibility.

00:52.440 --> 00:58.960
So my advice for you, especially if you are new to the CTE, that's try to not add a new CTE each time

00:58.960 --> 01:00.280
you are doing something new.

01:00.280 --> 01:05.880
And I saw it a lot like for each new calculation, for each new column, they jump immediately and create

01:05.880 --> 01:06.720
a new city.

01:06.760 --> 01:08.040
And what happens at the end?

01:08.080 --> 01:13.560
We can have like massive number of cities inside one query, and the developer thinks now everything

01:13.560 --> 01:15.680
is organized and easy to read.

01:15.680 --> 01:18.360
But believe me, it's exactly the opposite.

01:18.360 --> 01:24.560
If you open any code and you have a lot of kids, and especially if they are necessities, it is impossible

01:24.560 --> 01:26.240
to understand what is going on.

01:26.280 --> 01:31.440
Even if the developer liked describe each CTE and the task of the CTE, it's going to be really hard

01:31.440 --> 01:33.280
to understand and as well to read.

01:33.440 --> 01:38.720
If everything is like nested and you have like, I don't know, 20 seats in one query, so it's going

01:38.720 --> 01:40.840
to be impossible to read and to understand.

01:40.840 --> 01:45.480
And as well, you're going to be using a lot of memory and you might get bad performance.

01:45.480 --> 01:50.000
So my advice for you try always as you are creating new cities to think about.

01:50.000 --> 01:52.640
How about to merge two cities in one?

01:52.640 --> 01:58.920
So it is really always important to rethink and refactor your CTE in order to merge it into one and

01:58.920 --> 02:00.680
to reduce the number of seats.

02:00.720 --> 02:06.120
But now, if you ask me how many seats are okay in one query, well, I don't have a magic number for

02:06.160 --> 02:10.240
that, but normally I tend to say between 3 and 5 seats.

02:10.280 --> 02:11.200
It's fine.

02:11.200 --> 02:14.520
So it's going to be easy to understand and to read and so on.

02:14.520 --> 02:19.320
But once you get more than five seats, then you have to rethink your code.

02:19.360 --> 02:24.360
Maybe you have to create another complete query so you don't have to put everything in one query.

02:24.360 --> 02:25.720
So this is my advice for you.

02:25.720 --> 02:29.720
Try to not overuse the seats in your projects, not for each step.

02:29.880 --> 02:36.400
Always refactor the seats, consolidate them, and try to not have more than five cities in one query.

02:36.400 --> 02:37.800
So that's my advice for you.

02:37.840 --> 02:41.520
Be responsible using the seats and let's go back to our course.
